WEBVTT

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:07.966
[upbeat music playing]

00:00:21.772 --> 00:00:25.692
[Jerry’s voice]
<i>This is a 1977 Toyota FJ40 Land Cruiser</i>

00:00:25.776 --> 00:00:28.820
<i>in mustard yellow</i>
<i>with a black vinyl interior.</i>

00:00:29.404 --> 00:00:33.033
<i>The Land Cruiser.</i>
<i>“Built tough to get you there and back.”</i>

00:00:33.116 --> 00:00:35.160
<i>I love that they tell you</i>
<i>they’re gonna get you back.</i>

00:00:36.161 --> 00:00:37.996
<i>I’ve always liked these vehicles.</i>

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.959
<i>It’s basic, it’s classic, it’s durable,</i>
<i>it’s user-friendly.</i>

00:00:42.376 --> 00:00:46.755
<i>I love the way this rear window</i>
<i>kinda curves around the back.</i>

00:00:47.631 --> 00:00:50.300
<i>The wheels look like cut up paper plates.</i>

00:00:50.926 --> 00:00:54.638
<i>It just seems like a giant toy.</i>
<i>It’s got a Tonka look about it.</i>

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:58.183
<i>Some people take the Toyota logo</i>
<i>and repaint it to say “Tonka.”</i>

00:00:59.434 --> 00:01:03.564
<i>I like to imagine that I live in Africa,</i>
<i>and I’m doing important research,</i>

00:01:03.647 --> 00:01:07.067
<i>and this is the only vehicle I’ve got</i>
<i>to get everywhere in Africa,</i>

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:10.070
<i>and you can’t break down,</i>
<i>because it’s Africa.</i>

00:01:10.696 --> 00:01:13.740
<i>You know,</i>
<i>it’s got big wheels and high-cut fenders,</i>

00:01:13.824 --> 00:01:16.493
<i>so that you can go anywhere</i>
<i>and do anything.</i>

00:01:16.577 --> 00:01:19.454
<i>And even if you get stuck,</i>
<i>you got a winch to get out.</i>

00:01:19.538 --> 00:01:22.332
<i>Hey, wait a minute. I am stuck.</i>
<i>Stop the opening.</i>

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:32.509
-Let’s go. Come on.
-[Jerry laughing]

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.221
[Jerry’s voice]
<i>This approachable, no-nonsense vibe…</i>

00:01:36.305 --> 00:01:37.264
This is you, isn’t it?

00:01:37.347 --> 00:01:42.019
<i>…reminded me of my very special guest</i>
<i>this week, Miss Ellen DeGeneres.</i>

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:47.274
<i>Who’s also so friendly, and accessible,</i>
<i>and real, and fun to be with.</i>

00:01:47.357 --> 00:01:48.358
[both sigh]

00:01:50.235 --> 00:01:51.403
[Ellen on phone] <i>Hello?</i>

00:01:51.486 --> 00:01:52.613
[Jerry on phone] <i>Hello, Ellen.</i>

00:01:52.696 --> 00:01:54.906
[Ellen] <i>Jerry, hi. I didn’t expect you.</i>

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.868
[Jerry] <i>What year do you think it was</i>
<i>that we last saw each other?</i>

00:01:57.951 --> 00:02:01.455
[Ellen] <i>We played Ping-Pong</i>
<i>at the Comedy at the Hermosa Beach.</i>

00:02:01.538 --> 00:02:03.248
-[Jerry] <i>Really?</i>
-[Ellen] <i>That’s what I remember.</i>

00:02:03.332 --> 00:02:05.792
-[Jerry] <i>That’s a good memory.</i>
-[Ellen] <i>Looking forward to spending time.</i>

00:02:05.876 --> 00:02:07.628
-<i>Haven’t seen you in ages.</i>
-[Jerry] <i>On my way.</i>

00:02:12.507 --> 00:02:16.928
<i>I’m Jerry Seinfeld, and this is</i>
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:19.473
[brake ratchets]

00:02:19.556 --> 00:02:20.432
Wow.

00:02:20.515 --> 00:02:22.059
-[Jerry] Isn’t it cool?
-[Ellen] Yes.

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:23.018
Oh, my God.

00:02:23.101 --> 00:02:24.811
-It’s just got that “you” look.
-Fantastic.

00:02:24.895 --> 00:02:26.730
-Have you been on safari?
-No.

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:30.442
-You should do a safari.
-Okay. Something else I gotta do.

00:02:32.235 --> 00:02:35.238
[Ellen] I like that you put your
turn signal on from the driveway.

00:02:35.322 --> 00:02:37.616
-I love driving, and that’s part of it.
-I see.

00:02:42.287 --> 00:02:46.166
Your talk show, you can’t have imagined
that would be as successful as it’s been.

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:47.959
I don’t know. It’s so funny, because I…

00:02:48.043 --> 00:02:52.172
I mean, I still don’t get it.
I still don’t get my appeal. You know?

00:02:52.255 --> 00:02:53.423
-You don’t?
-No.

00:02:53.507 --> 00:02:54.800
I mean, I really don’t.

00:02:56.385 --> 00:02:59.846
Well, how the hell do you run all this,
if you don’t understand?

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:03.141
I just go along with it.
You know? It’s like “all right.”

00:03:03.225 --> 00:03:05.143
What would you like your appeal to be?

00:03:05.227 --> 00:03:08.313
What would you like people to think
when they see you?

00:03:08.397 --> 00:03:10.982
I’m a good person. I’m a nice person.

00:03:11.066 --> 00:03:13.276
-Nobody’s paying to see a nice person.
-You go right ahead.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:14.444
-Really?
-No.

00:03:19.783 --> 00:03:21.868
I was obsessed with comedians,

00:03:21.952 --> 00:03:24.871
but I never thought
I was gonna be a comedian.

00:03:24.955 --> 00:03:27.207
What was the hinge that it happened?

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:31.002
Someone asked me to do something
to help raise money,

00:03:31.086 --> 00:03:34.005
and someone said:
“You get up onstage, and be funny.”

00:03:34.089 --> 00:03:36.341
-So I got up onstage.
-Right.

00:03:36.425 --> 00:03:41.096
I brought a bag, a Burger King Whopper,
and fries, and a shake.

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:43.473
And I started a sentence, I said,

00:03:44.182 --> 00:03:47.519
“I’m so sorry. This is the first time I’ve
ever been onstage, and I’m so nervous,

00:03:47.602 --> 00:03:49.980
so I didn’t eat all day,
and I realized that on the way over,

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:51.982
so I stopped to get this.
I hope you don’t mind.”

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:55.026
And then I started a sentence, saying,
“I don’t know if you’ve ever noticed,

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.195
but when people…” and then I took a bite.

00:03:57.279 --> 00:03:59.364
And then I put my finger up, and said…

00:03:59.448 --> 00:04:02.534
You know how people do that? Like,
why start a sentence, and then, you know.

00:04:02.617 --> 00:04:05.829
-So I literally ate the entire…
-That’s funny.

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:08.290
I started the same sentence
over and over again,

00:04:08.373 --> 00:04:11.042
and finished the entire Whopper and fries.

00:04:11.126 --> 00:04:14.254
And then said:
“I’m so sorry, that’s my time.”

00:04:14.337 --> 00:04:17.424
-That is a funny bit.
-That’s what I did for the first time.

00:04:19.217 --> 00:04:23.430
And then I had to start writing material.
Then I had to, like, come up with ideas.

00:04:24.014 --> 00:04:26.933
And then the thing that changed my life,
of course,

00:04:27.017 --> 00:04:29.770
is my girlfriend was killed
in a car accident,

00:04:29.853 --> 00:04:31.646
and I just kept thinking,

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:36.193
“Why is this 23-year-old girl
that I loved,

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:38.028
you know, just… gone?”

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:41.323
And I just thought, “What if we could
just pick up the phone, and call up God,

00:04:41.406 --> 00:04:43.700
and ask these questions,
and have a conversation?”

00:04:43.784 --> 00:04:46.077
’Cause people pray all the time,
but really,

00:04:46.161 --> 00:04:49.080
wouldn’t it be amazing
if you could have a connection?

00:04:49.164 --> 00:04:51.291
-Yeah.
-So, I literally started writing,

00:04:51.374 --> 00:04:54.127
that it would ring for a long time.
I didn’t mean for it to be funny.

00:04:54.211 --> 00:04:58.465
But I just thought it would ring
a long time. It’s big. It’s a big place.

00:04:58.548 --> 00:05:02.803
And then finally, when God answers,
I’d be put on hold, like…

00:05:02.886 --> 00:05:06.264
And then it’s “Onward, Christian
Soldiers”, but it’s not a tape, it’s live.

00:05:06.348 --> 00:05:10.060
And I literally wrote it
without putting my pen down,

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:11.937
and I finished it, and I was like,

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:14.064
“Oh, my God,
I’m gonna do this on Johnny Carson,

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:16.858
and I’m gonna be the first woman
in the history of the show

00:05:16.942 --> 00:05:19.611
to be called over
to sit down with Johnny Carson.”

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:23.532
And five years later, I was
the first woman in the history of the show

00:05:23.615 --> 00:05:25.909
to sit down with Johnny Carson, and…

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:27.494
Because of the phone call to God.

00:05:27.577 --> 00:05:29.204
-Wow.
-I know.

00:05:29.287 --> 00:05:30.539
-Crazy.
-Crazy.

00:05:33.667 --> 00:05:36.503
Have you ever had
any other kinds of visions like that,

00:05:36.586 --> 00:05:38.588
of things that were gonna happen,
that happened?

00:05:38.672 --> 00:05:39.881
I think being…

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:43.468
famous was really important to me,

00:05:43.552 --> 00:05:46.972
and I do believe that that intention
was set early on.

00:05:47.055 --> 00:05:50.725
And I was raised Christian Science,
so nobody really acknowledged

00:05:50.809 --> 00:05:52.978
-pain or anything, you know?
-Right.

00:05:53.061 --> 00:05:57.941
There was very little
expression of emotions, or depth, or love.

00:05:58.024 --> 00:05:58.942
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:05:59.985 --> 00:06:02.153
You know?
But my brother was famous before me.

00:06:02.237 --> 00:06:06.032
My brother was on <i>Saturday Night Live</i>.
He was Mr. Hands for Mr. Bill.

00:06:06.116 --> 00:06:08.535
-He was?
-Yeah. He helped create Mr. Bill.

00:06:08.618 --> 00:06:09.828
Wow, I didn’t know that.

00:06:13.248 --> 00:06:14.916
[Ellen] You’re an excellent driver.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.460
Thank you.
That’s one of my favorite compliments.

00:06:17.544 --> 00:06:19.421
Not one crunch or anything.

00:06:19.504 --> 00:06:21.464
-Not one “krrg.”
-Thank you.

00:06:23.758 --> 00:06:25.135
-What do you need?
-My wallet.

00:06:25.969 --> 00:06:27.887
Losing your wallet is the scariest thing.

00:06:27.971 --> 00:06:29.723
I don’t have a wallet.
I just keep it loose.

00:06:29.806 --> 00:06:31.891
I just keep the cash loose in my pants.

00:06:31.975 --> 00:06:35.520
“Loose in my pants,”
that’s the Louisiana coming out.

00:06:35.604 --> 00:06:38.189
Yeah. “Y’all” and “loose in my pants.”

00:06:38.273 --> 00:06:40.984
I don’t know that I’ve used that statement
before, “loose in my pants.”

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:42.193
I like it loose in my pants.

00:06:42.277 --> 00:06:44.195
[laughs] Well, that may be
the name of my special.

00:06:44.279 --> 00:06:45.113
[laughs]

00:06:46.615 --> 00:06:48.366
-Hi, how are you? Good to see you.
-Hi, how are you?

00:06:52.829 --> 00:06:54.789
-[Jerry] Do you like coffee?
-[Ellen] I do.

00:06:55.582 --> 00:06:58.918
-I bet you want coffee.
-I would love a coffee, yes.

00:07:00.754 --> 00:07:04.758
When I lost my sitcom,
I didn’t work for three years solid.

00:07:04.841 --> 00:07:07.052
Until I got the show, until the talk show.

00:07:07.135 --> 00:07:09.846
Right. So when you say
you weren’t working, what were you doing?

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:11.890
I was in Ojai,

00:07:12.349 --> 00:07:16.770
like, literally bitter and sad
and angry and… You know?

00:07:16.853 --> 00:07:19.773
Like, how,
like, did this change everything,

00:07:19.856 --> 00:07:21.816
just by me being honest
and saying I’m gay?

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:24.486
Like, why is this such a shock to people?

00:07:24.569 --> 00:07:26.988
-And why is it such a big deal?
-Uh-huh.

00:07:27.072 --> 00:07:32.243
But literally, I went on tour, and I would
say 90 percent of the audience was gay.

00:07:32.327 --> 00:07:34.329
And some brave straight people went.

00:07:34.412 --> 00:07:38.416
And all the gay people really thought
I was doing some kind of gay pride tour.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:39.709
Right, right.

00:07:39.793 --> 00:07:42.170
So I was making fun of the fact
that I was their leader now.

00:07:42.253 --> 00:07:45.006
And I was like, “I am not your leader.
I’m not anybody’s leader.”

00:07:46.007 --> 00:07:47.634
I act like that around firemen.

00:07:47.717 --> 00:07:50.553
-I love firemen.
-Firemen are very exciting to see.

00:07:50.637 --> 00:07:54.265
A fireman stopped me the other day,
I got so excited to talk to him.

00:07:54.349 --> 00:07:57.560
They’re great. They don’t carry guns.
They run into danger.

00:07:57.644 --> 00:07:59.396
And they get a lot of girls too,
don’t they?

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:01.022
-Do they?
-I think so.

00:08:01.106 --> 00:08:02.524
They had that calendar.

00:08:05.026 --> 00:08:07.696
I know you don’t like penises,
but even if you did…

00:08:07.779 --> 00:08:08.738
I’m not saying I don’t.

00:08:08.822 --> 00:08:10.532
-You don’t?
-It’s not that I don’t.

00:08:10.615 --> 00:08:12.701
You don’t? You don’t.

00:08:12.784 --> 00:08:15.745
-Or you do?
-Well, they’re fine. I don’t have a…

00:08:15.829 --> 00:08:18.832
That’s how I feel about it.
Purely aesthetics.

00:08:19.666 --> 00:08:20.875
The penis, it’s…

00:08:21.876 --> 00:08:23.420
It has a function.

00:08:23.503 --> 00:08:26.464
-It’s clever in the way it works.
-Yes.

00:08:26.548 --> 00:08:29.050
It’s kinda like one of these
expandable strollers.

00:08:29.134 --> 00:08:30.093
Right.

00:08:30.969 --> 00:08:34.431
Because the world is such a scary place
right now, like, in so many ways.

00:08:34.514 --> 00:08:36.141
And you have children,
and there’s North Korea,

00:08:36.224 --> 00:08:38.184
and there’s fires, there’s storms…

00:08:38.268 --> 00:08:41.438
It just overwhelms me with, like, dread.

00:08:41.521 --> 00:08:43.606
-Like, do you go to those places?
-No.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:44.774
-You don’t?
-No.

00:08:45.358 --> 00:08:47.193
You know, my attitude is

00:08:47.277 --> 00:08:52.198
that each generation kinda gets this thing
dumped in your lap to deal with.

00:08:52.282 --> 00:08:53.908
Right. If I talk to your kids,

00:08:53.992 --> 00:08:56.286
when I meet them,
I’ll ask them what they think.

00:08:56.369 --> 00:08:58.079
I don’t really care what they think.

00:08:58.163 --> 00:08:59.998
See? That’s my point.

00:09:00.582 --> 00:09:02.667
You chose to bring them into this world,

00:09:02.751 --> 00:09:04.002
so it’s your job to…

00:09:04.085 --> 00:09:07.172
You say “chose.” I was just
fooling around like everybody else, okay?

00:09:07.255 --> 00:09:09.257
-So let’s not put it all on me.
-Okay.

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:10.967
-It’s a lot of fun.
-Yeah.

00:09:11.051 --> 00:09:12.969
-Having kids?
-Yeah. It’s a lot of fun.

00:09:13.053 --> 00:09:15.972
If you look at it as I do,
without any sense of responsibility.

00:09:16.056 --> 00:09:16.890
Exactly.

00:09:21.519 --> 00:09:22.812
What do you think of this theory?

00:09:23.396 --> 00:09:26.066
I think you’re clearly
a very empathetic person.

00:09:26.149 --> 00:09:27.067
Yes.

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:29.194
Do you think the size of people’s eyes

00:09:29.277 --> 00:09:32.322
has something to do with
how aware and sensitive they are?

00:09:32.405 --> 00:09:33.782
I know that I have large ears.

00:09:33.865 --> 00:09:37.035
Racist people always have little eyes,
little mouths, not taking in a lot.

00:09:37.118 --> 00:09:38.661
-Really?
-What do you think of that theory?

00:09:38.745 --> 00:09:41.164
Well, first of all,
I feel better about my ears now.

00:09:44.292 --> 00:09:48.755
Are you at all upset at this point of
what you wore on your first <i>Tonight Show</i>?

00:09:48.838 --> 00:09:51.091
You know who guided me?

00:09:51.174 --> 00:09:52.801
And that was a big mistake. Sinbad.

00:09:52.884 --> 00:09:55.220
-And I was following Sinbad’s advice.
-[Jerry laughs]

00:09:55.303 --> 00:09:56.471
That’s how insecure I was.

00:09:57.889 --> 00:09:59.849
All these other things you do,
I can’t do any of them.

00:09:59.933 --> 00:10:01.810
You could be a talk show host tomorrow.

00:10:01.893 --> 00:10:03.436
I can’t do it.

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.522
-Yes, you can.
-I can’t talk. No.

00:10:05.605 --> 00:10:07.732
I can’t talk
to people I’m not interested in.

00:10:07.816 --> 00:10:09.734
You could do, like, six a year,
or something.

00:10:09.818 --> 00:10:11.236
That’s what I’m doing.

00:10:12.278 --> 00:10:14.072
-I like a lot of the Netflix stuff.
-Right.

00:10:14.155 --> 00:10:17.158
And now they don’t even give you time
to make a decision. You’re watching one…

00:10:17.242 --> 00:10:20.036
It used to be, like, 12 seconds,
and now it’s like four seconds,

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:22.205
-and it goes right to the next one.
-Right.

00:10:22.288 --> 00:10:25.250
It’s like feeding a goose pâté.
You know the way they make pâté?

00:10:25.333 --> 00:10:27.168
It’s a horrible thing to think about.

00:10:27.252 --> 00:10:28.795
-What they do to gooses?
-Yeah.

00:10:28.878 --> 00:10:29.838
-It’s horrible.
-Yes.

00:10:29.921 --> 00:10:31.464
That’s what I feel like it is.

00:10:31.548 --> 00:10:33.216
By the way, they don’t feed them pâté.

00:10:33.299 --> 00:10:36.094
No, they make pâté…

00:10:36.594 --> 00:10:38.513
-From overfeeding them.
-It’s horrible.

00:10:38.596 --> 00:10:40.682
-It’s the cruelest thing ever.
-Yeah.

00:10:40.765 --> 00:10:43.393
-I hope you’re happy.
-Just think about them feeding ’em pâté.

00:10:43.852 --> 00:10:46.062
-Because that’s even crueler.
-Yeah.

00:10:46.646 --> 00:10:49.065
We’re now gonna give you
the food you’re gonna be.

00:10:49.899 --> 00:10:50.900
This is delicious.

00:10:50.984 --> 00:10:55.113
So, you mean Nancy,
who used to be next door to me…

00:10:55.196 --> 00:10:56.030
Right.

00:10:56.364 --> 00:10:57.198
Oh, God.

00:10:57.949 --> 00:10:59.075
You people are horrible.

00:10:59.159 --> 00:11:00.952
Yep. Human beings are horrible.

00:11:01.035 --> 00:11:02.579
They’re horrible, but

00:11:03.246 --> 00:11:05.039
the problem with human beings

00:11:05.123 --> 00:11:09.043
is as much as you may want
to dislike or disapprove of them,

00:11:09.794 --> 00:11:12.172
then they just do one wonderful thing.

00:11:12.255 --> 00:11:16.009
And they get you back in again.
It’s an abusive relationship we have here.

00:11:16.092 --> 00:11:18.094
-With our own species.
-Right.

00:11:18.678 --> 00:11:22.599
We keep believing
they’re gonna stop doing this, right?

00:11:22.682 --> 00:11:24.183
He’s not gonna beat me again.

00:11:24.976 --> 00:11:25.810
Wow.

00:11:26.603 --> 00:11:28.563
-Right? How do you like this premise?
-Yeah.

00:11:28.646 --> 00:11:33.401
So we go from pâté
to someone beating their wife.

00:11:33.484 --> 00:11:34.527
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:11:34.611 --> 00:11:36.279
-You know that’s how they do it.
-Yeah.

00:11:36.362 --> 00:11:39.449
He is an abusive husband,
but then he’s so nice.

00:11:39.532 --> 00:11:42.076
-He’s like “Hey, here, have some pâté.”
-Yeah. Have some pâté.

00:11:43.703 --> 00:11:47.749
And now we’re calling it “pah-tay.”
It’s turned into “pah-tay.”

00:11:51.336 --> 00:11:54.672
If you did smoke,
what brand would you like?

00:11:55.256 --> 00:11:56.215
Maybe a Parliament.

00:11:56.299 --> 00:11:58.384
Oh, really? With the quarter inch…?

00:11:58.468 --> 00:11:59.844
-The recessed filter?
-Yeah.

00:11:59.928 --> 00:12:03.139
Do you miss cigarette advertising
on TV at all?

00:12:03.222 --> 00:12:04.474
No, I don’t miss it.

00:12:04.557 --> 00:12:06.059
-I do.
-Really?

00:12:06.976 --> 00:12:11.272
I just like that it was an interesting
problem that they had to solve.

00:12:11.356 --> 00:12:15.318
To make these people look like fun people
I wanna be, but they’re smokers.

00:12:15.401 --> 00:12:16.402
Which is so gross.

00:12:16.486 --> 00:12:18.655
-Right.
-But we’re gonna put them on a sailboat,

00:12:18.738 --> 00:12:20.907
and we’re gonna dress them
in a blue stripe.

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:22.909
With, you know, little white shorts.

00:12:22.992 --> 00:12:24.827
-That would be Salem.
-Salem.

00:12:26.371 --> 00:12:30.625
I’ve never seen anyone drink
so much coffee in my entire life.

00:12:30.708 --> 00:12:32.669
It’s too much. It doesn’t affect you?

00:12:32.752 --> 00:12:33.878
Of course it affects me.

00:12:34.545 --> 00:12:35.797
See how fast…

00:12:37.173 --> 00:12:38.007
Wow.

00:12:39.175 --> 00:12:42.679
I thought I would have kids. When I was
younger I really wanted to have a baby.

00:12:42.762 --> 00:12:45.139
I thought I would adopt.
I never thought I would have a baby,

00:12:45.223 --> 00:12:46.724
but I thought I would want kids.

00:12:46.808 --> 00:12:49.602
-You still could.
-No, Portia doesn’t want kids.

00:12:49.686 --> 00:12:52.397
She doesn’t like the squealing
when they’re in a pool.

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:56.734
That high-pitched squealing of joy
that they have.

00:12:56.818 --> 00:12:58.194
-She doesn’t like that.
-All right.

00:12:58.277 --> 00:13:01.364
But she wants lots more horses,
which I don’t.

00:13:01.447 --> 00:13:03.616
Right. What does that got to do with you?

00:13:04.367 --> 00:13:07.078
Well, you know. It’s a lot of horses.

00:13:07.161 --> 00:13:09.414
-You don’t have to take care of them.
-No.

00:13:09.998 --> 00:13:11.332
Do you want my advice?

00:13:11.916 --> 00:13:12.917
No… Okay.

00:13:17.672 --> 00:13:19.340
-Anyways, yeah.
-That was a great delivery.

00:13:19.424 --> 00:13:21.009
You included the “no” in the “yes.”

00:13:21.092 --> 00:13:22.593
-That’s why it was so funny.
-I know.

00:13:23.219 --> 00:13:24.053
My advice…

00:13:31.269 --> 00:13:34.105
-I’m not gonna say “pah-tay.”
-Uh-uh.

00:13:34.188 --> 00:13:38.109
My advice is just keep giving her horses,
until she can’t stand them.

00:13:38.192 --> 00:13:40.737
Okay. That’s not okay. Really?

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:43.281
-It’s my general wife advice anyway.
-All right.

00:13:43.364 --> 00:13:44.991
Always give a wife whatever she wants.

00:13:45.783 --> 00:13:47.994
She will eventually see.
“I didn’t really want that.”

00:13:48.077 --> 00:13:49.996
-You know she doesn’t want it.
-Right.

00:13:50.079 --> 00:13:52.040
-Let them find out on their own.
-Okay.

00:13:55.043 --> 00:13:57.503
-Do you have cats and dogs?
-Two cats, two dogs.

00:13:57.587 --> 00:13:59.213
-What kind of dogs?
-Dachshunds.

00:13:59.964 --> 00:14:01.549
-They’re cute.
-Stupid.

00:14:01.632 --> 00:14:02.675
-Are they?
-Very stupid.

00:14:02.759 --> 00:14:04.927
-Did you get them both at the same time?
-No.

00:14:05.011 --> 00:14:07.305
So you knew it was like that,
and got a second one?

00:14:07.388 --> 00:14:09.849
-I don’t do anything.
-I see. Okay.

00:14:09.932 --> 00:14:11.642
-You know how you have favorites?
-Yeah.

00:14:11.726 --> 00:14:14.479
You don’t feel as bad about that
with animals.

00:14:14.562 --> 00:14:16.939
-You don’t wanna do that with kids.
-You don’t wanna do that with kids.

00:14:17.023 --> 00:14:19.734
But I’m sure you do.
I mean, you’ve gotta have a favorite kid.

00:14:19.817 --> 00:14:20.777
-No.
-You have to.

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:21.903
No, you really don’t.

00:14:22.528 --> 00:14:24.614
-They’re all different. I know that.
-Yeah, of course.

00:14:24.697 --> 00:14:27.283
You know, love is not rational.

00:14:27.367 --> 00:14:29.535
You could say,
“Well, I love this person, because…”

00:14:29.619 --> 00:14:30.953
But that’s not really the reason.

00:14:31.037 --> 00:14:33.915
There is no reason. You just… You feel it.

00:14:33.998 --> 00:14:36.793
-And you have no control over it.
-No, and you have no control over it.

00:14:36.876 --> 00:14:40.880
Which is a weird thing, because that’s why
I think people get themselves into trouble

00:14:40.963 --> 00:14:43.841
in relationships with people that,
as you said, beat them.

00:14:43.925 --> 00:14:45.259
-Like, you…
-Yes.

00:14:45.343 --> 00:14:47.553
What do you mean, “as I said”?
They do beat them.

00:14:47.637 --> 00:14:49.013
-Are they funny?
-Yeah.

00:14:49.097 --> 00:14:51.265
-Who’s the funniest?
-I am.

00:14:53.101 --> 00:14:54.727
-Let’s go on a tour.
-Let’s go.

00:14:54.811 --> 00:14:55.770
All right.

00:14:56.813 --> 00:14:59.273
Yeah. Have one more sip of your coffee.

00:14:59.357 --> 00:15:00.983
Wow. I mean…

00:15:01.067 --> 00:15:04.153
You’re looking at me like I got
a bent spoon here and I’m heating it up.

00:15:04.237 --> 00:15:06.781
It’s the irony of if you die
from too much coffee.

00:15:06.864 --> 00:15:08.282
-Who cares?
-This is your show.

00:15:08.366 --> 00:15:10.743
Haven’t we have enough
of whatever the hell I do?

00:15:10.827 --> 00:15:13.329
-Come on. It’s enough.
-No, we don’t wanna lose you.

00:15:13.413 --> 00:15:14.247
You’re sweet.

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:15.706
[Jerry] That’s Jane Fonda.

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:18.084
-What kind is it?
-It’s a Ferrari 250.

00:15:18.167 --> 00:15:19.961
[no audible speech]

00:15:24.382 --> 00:15:26.259
-I like drug stores.
-Yeah, me too.

00:15:27.218 --> 00:15:28.886
How about this? You wanna feel like that?

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:30.972
-What? Yes.
-I don’t think I wanna feel like that.

00:15:31.055 --> 00:15:33.099
-I don’t like…
-You don’t have Jean Naté?

00:15:33.182 --> 00:15:34.684
The only ladies that live around here…

00:15:34.767 --> 00:15:36.352
-It’s an old lady…
-I know.

00:15:36.436 --> 00:15:38.396
-I would think it’d be here.
-It’s not an old lady place.

00:15:38.479 --> 00:15:40.189
Don’t act like where I live
is an old lady…

00:15:40.273 --> 00:15:41.983
There’s a lot of young people
who live here.

00:15:42.066 --> 00:15:43.860
Look at all these people.
They’re all young.

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:45.653
No, they’re not.

00:15:46.446 --> 00:15:47.738
I don’t have enough.

00:15:47.822 --> 00:15:50.741
Don’t you anticipate when you shoot these
things you’re gonna spend money?

00:15:50.825 --> 00:15:53.035
No. I told you, I anticipate nothing.

00:15:53.119 --> 00:15:55.496
I don’t care what happens.
I’ll deal with it when it happens.

00:15:56.998 --> 00:15:58.583
You’re right.
There’s no older people here.

00:15:58.666 --> 00:16:01.252
No older people at all. It’s all kids.

00:16:03.045 --> 00:16:05.131
-Hey, where’s the keys?
-I don’t know.

00:16:05.214 --> 00:16:08.092
Do you ever get nervous about
people scratching your car, or anything?

00:16:08.176 --> 00:16:09.051
-No.
-Actually you do,

00:16:09.135 --> 00:16:11.929
because my friend Nacho said
he was driving with you…

00:16:12.013 --> 00:16:13.055
That’s different.

00:16:14.390 --> 00:16:15.850
The keys are not in the car.

00:16:16.517 --> 00:16:18.728
See, I told you
you shouldn’t leave the keys in the car.

00:16:18.811 --> 00:16:21.105
-Looking for a key.
-I didn’t touch ’em.

00:16:21.189 --> 00:16:22.023
I didn’t either.

00:16:22.106 --> 00:16:24.859
-You didn’t put ’em in your pocket?
-Maybe I did, but I don’t…

00:16:25.443 --> 00:16:28.112
I must have. But I don’t have them.
I swear.

00:16:28.821 --> 00:16:29.822
They’re not in…

00:16:29.906 --> 00:16:32.825
It was to teach you a lesson.
And you should always…

00:16:32.909 --> 00:16:34.827
I could’ve let that go longer.

00:16:35.411 --> 00:16:36.662
If you had any balls.

00:16:37.413 --> 00:16:38.289
Which you don’t.

00:16:38.372 --> 00:16:40.333
I should’ve let it go really, really long.

00:16:40.416 --> 00:16:42.043
I don’t think it was going anyplace funny.

00:16:42.126 --> 00:16:44.754
-Had we gone longer…
-How’d you get them out? How’d you do it?

00:16:44.837 --> 00:16:46.839
When you walked around the car.

00:16:46.923 --> 00:16:47.757
That’s funny.

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:49.175
You just said it wasn’t funny.

00:16:49.258 --> 00:16:50.885
No, the way you did it is funny.

00:16:50.968 --> 00:16:52.678
What you did is not funny.

00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:55.806
Everyone is so friendly here,

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:59.435
and you never hear helicopters,
you never hear sirens.

00:16:59.519 --> 00:17:01.771
-It’s so great.
-I’m so glad you like it.

00:17:02.355 --> 00:17:03.731
That sounded sarcastic.

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:05.942
A little bit.

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:14.116
-This thing is cool, isn’t it?
-Yeah.

00:17:14.784 --> 00:17:16.869
’Cause usually there’s the vents up here.

00:17:16.953 --> 00:17:19.205
And there’s a thing
where you could even open it.

00:17:22.875 --> 00:17:24.961
-[man] I love you!
-[Ellen laughs]

00:17:25.044 --> 00:17:25.878
Him or me?

00:17:25.962 --> 00:17:27.630
[hollers, indistinct]

00:17:27.713 --> 00:17:28.756
Both of us, I think.

00:17:31.259 --> 00:17:32.635
Sorry you had to turn.

00:17:46.315 --> 00:17:48.859
[Jerry] Can you imagine how irresistible
it would have been

00:17:48.943 --> 00:17:50.945
for somebody like me to have met…

00:17:51.028 --> 00:17:53.030
’Cause I didn’t even know
people were named Portia.

00:17:53.114 --> 00:17:55.950
Met a girl named Portia.
You know what that would’ve done to me?

00:17:56.033 --> 00:17:57.410
-You would’ve married her.
-Yeah.

00:17:57.493 --> 00:17:59.495
But then she wouldn’t wanna marry you.

00:17:59.579 --> 00:18:01.289
No. The other name…

00:18:01.372 --> 00:18:03.833
Alec Baldwin’s wife’s name is Hilaria.

00:18:03.916 --> 00:18:04.792
Which I thought…

00:18:04.875 --> 00:18:06.085
-Is that right?
-Yeah.

00:18:06.168 --> 00:18:07.128
I didn’t know that.

00:18:07.211 --> 00:18:09.338
I thought,
what a great name for a comedian’s wife.

00:18:09.422 --> 00:18:10.798
-Yes.
-“Hilaria.”